// src/v4/extdeps/formats/csv.dag
//
// Scope: CSV (Comma-Separated Values) data format model — direction-agnostic.
// Anchor: https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc4180 (RFC 4180)
//
// Owns:
//   - CsvDocument { width: PositiveInt, header: Option<Row>, records: List<Row> }
//   - Row = a field sequence whose length is STRUCTURALLY == width
//     (rectangularity is an invariant of the type, not a convention —
//     RFC 4180 §1: "each line should contain the same number of fields".
//     `List<List<String>>` is rejected: it admits ragged records and
//     header-width drift, an illegal state. Ragged input is a parse
//     Diagnostic, never a representable CsvDocument — fail-closed.)
//   - parse(String, Dialect) -> Result<CsvDocument, Diagnostic>
//     (width fixed by header if present, else by the first record; any
//     record whose arity != width is a Diagnostic, not a truncation/pad)
//   - emit(CsvDocument, Dialect) -> String
//   - Dialect { delimiter: Char, quote: Char, escape: Option<Char>, line_terminator: String }
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/node.dag, std/diagnostic.dag, std/collection.dag, std/text.dag
//
// Status: scaffold — fill per TASKS.md T-4.6
// Brief: see BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md; this file's I/O contract is the immutable
// contract for the worker. Splitting this file requires explicit operator
// ratification (substrate extension = stop signal).

module v4.extdeps.formats.csv


import v4.std.nat { Nat }
import v4.std.text { Char }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (read before validating against the anchor)
//
// CANONICAL-MIRROR (T-4.6 fan-out). EXACT-mirrors the ratified canonical
// src/v4/extdeps/formats/json.dag seam (PR #3184, T-4 mgr seam-review
// ratification msg_f5971a8a): the format's value carriers + node.dag A1
// discipline + the Practice-4 five-pattern ledger where a sum applies;
// the parse/emit carrier seam is the ratified corrected seam #3 —
// fail-closed BOTH directions: `csv_parse : String -> Outcome<CsvDocument>`
// AND **`csv_emit : CsvDocument -> Outcome<String>`** (with dialect
// supplied as an explicit parameter — the CSV surface is NOT pure
// `String -> …` like JSON; the ratified `Outcome<T>` carrier is still the
// ONE D1 carrier from std/diagnostic.dag DECISIONS item **I**). Operations
// deferred-with-named-owner. (Single authority = the Deferred (1) block
// below; this summary states it, the block specifies it — no divergence.)
//
// L-2 VERSION PIN (C5 / extdeps discipline): RFC 4180 is the normative
// CSV grammar for this file; `datatracker.ietf.org` HTML for RFC 4180 is
// the checkable anchor revision. Spec drift = visible C1 edit to this
// header's Anchor URL + receipt note.
//
// RECTANGULARITY — OPERATOR-PENDING STRUCTURAL CARRIER (T-4.6 Lane C
// brief; supersedes the immutable header's "STRUCTURALLY == width" claim
// for the PRE-RECONCILE body below — tracked-not-silent, csv-class). RFC
// 4180 §1 states each record "should" contain the same number of fields;
// the fail-closed PARSE profile the operator ratified for gunbc is
// STRICTER (same arity across header + records, no ragged representable
// document). A type-level "`Row` length == `width`" indexed family is NOT
// expressible in the settled v2/v4 substrate today (no length-indexed
// vector refinement — the same class of gap as toml.dag's `NonEmptyStr`
// header vs `Map<String, …>` carrier). The delivered model is therefore
// HONESTLY SCOPED like yaml.dag's `YamlMapping` §3.2.1.3 note: `CsvRow`
// carries `List<String>`; **arity == `width` is NOT structurally enforced
// by the carriers** — it is a PARSE-TIME + downstream-validation invariant
// owned by the deferred `csv_parse` body (🟡 (2) below), fail-closed
// `Rejected` on mismatch, never silent padding/truncation (INVARIANTS P3).
// Full structural rectangularity (illegal ragged states unrepresentable)
// waits the operator-pinned **length-indexed row / PositiveInt width**
// substrate + the batched immutable-header reconcile where `Row`/`width`/
// `PositiveInt` prose matches the machine-readable carriers (operator-
// tier; worker does not edit the immutable header block above).
//
// DECLARATIVE-NOW / OPERATIONS-DEFERRED-WITH-OWNER. Modeled NOW: `CsvRow`,
// `CsvDocument`, `CsvDialect`, and the escape/header presence sums.
// DEFERRED (🟡, named owner + dissolution trigger, NEVER improvised, NEVER
// a bodiless `fn` — the frozen v2 seed rejects bodiless `fn`, DECISIONS
// PARSE-1): `csv_parse`/`csv_emit` bodies, dialect validation (delimiter
// must not equal quote except where the deferred grammar explicitly
// permits RFC edge cases), and Char-range validation on dialect code
// points (std/text.dag `Char` scaffold). Verified pattern: std/collection.
// dag #3169 + canonical json.dag #3184.
//
// CARRIER SEAM — fail-closed BOTH directions (authority = Deferred (1)):
//   - `csv_parse : (source: String, dialect: CsvDialect) -> Outcome<CsvDocument>`
//   - `csv_emit  : (doc: CsvDocument, dialect: CsvDialect) -> Outcome<String>`
// Immutable scaffold header's `parse(String, Dialect) -> Result<…>` /
// `emit(CsvDocument, Dialect) -> String` lines: the `Result<…, Diagnostic>`
// prose denotes `Outcome<…>` per DECISIONS **I**; the `-> String` emit line
// is the superseded total-emit shape — operator-tier header-reconcile
// (csv-class; tracked-not-silent in Deferred (1)), same json.dag #3184
// finding.
//
// HEADER `Option<Char>` / `Option<Row>` — delivered as explicit sums
// (`CsvEscapeSpec`, `CsvHeaderPresence`) per v4 P2 discipline (diagnostic.
// dag: no bare `Option<Node>` class of encodings for semantic presence).
// The immutable header's `Option<…>` spelling is the conceptual contract;
// the mechanical carriers below are the reconciled explicit form pending
// a literal header edit batch.
//
// `Bool`/`String` kernel-ambient; `List`/`Map` kernel containers (STRUCTURE.
// md). `Nat`/`Char` are imported as modeled authorities (width metadata;
// dialect code points).


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// CsvEscapeSpec — CSV Dialect escape character presence (immutable-header
// `escape: Option<Char>`).
type CsvEscapeSpec
  = CsvEscapeChar { escape: Char }
  | CsvEscapeAbsent


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// CsvHeaderPresence — optional header row (immutable-header `header:
// Option<Row>`). `HeaderPresent.row` is ONE logical header record; RFC
// profile: its arity participates in the deferred rectangularity check
// against every body row (🟡 (2)).
type CsvHeaderPresence
  = HeaderPresent { row: CsvRow }
  | HeaderAbsent


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// CsvDialect — RFC 4180–oriented delimiter/quote/escape/line-break
// parameters. Anchor: RFC 4180 (comma default; double-quote field
// enclosure). **Field-level escape rules** inside quoted fields are
// parse-body work (🟡 Deferred (1)); the carriers hold the parameters only.
type CsvDialect {
  delimiter: Char,
  quote: Char,
  escape: CsvEscapeSpec,
  line_terminator: String,
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// CsvRow — one logical record as an ordered field list. The immutable
// header names this authority `Row`; v4 declares it as `CsvRow` for grep
// stability (operator header-reconcile may alias the spelling).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4) — N/A (this is a `Conj` record, not a
// sum). The row is not a recursive JSON-like value space; it is a single
// product carrying `List<String>` (A1: recursion is through `List`, not
// through `CsvRow`).
type CsvRow {
  fields: List<String>,
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// CsvDocument — a header slot + body records + the logical field-count
// metadata `width` from the immutable scaffold header (`PositiveInt` in
// prose). Delivered `width: Nat` is the honest counting carrier (Peano
// `Nat` from std/nat.dag — the same non-negative counting discipline as
// the rest of the numeric tower); **strict positive width** (`PositiveInt`)
// is refinement-substrate operator-pending — surfaced here, not fabricated
// as a pretend `PositiveInt` type. Until refinement lands, arity checks
// against `width == Zero` are part of the same deferred parse authority
// as rectangularity (🟡 (2)).
//
// RECTANGULARITY (HONEST SCOPE — see modeling notes): `records` MAY
// structurally contain rows whose `fields` length differs from `width`
// AND from each other; such a value is either (a) not constructible from
// a conforming deferred `csv_parse`, or (b) a producer bug / invalid
// manual construction — emit MUST fail-closed (`Outcome`) per json.dag
// seam #3, never fabricate a normalizing CSV artifact.
type CsvDocument {
  width: Nat,
  header: CsvHeaderPresence,
  records: List<CsvRow>,
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Deferred — TRACKED SCAFFOLD (🟡). Operations + dialect grammar ride
// substrate that is scaffold today. NOT authored here — not improvised
// bodies, not bodiless `fn` (DECISIONS PARSE-1).
//
// (1) `csv_parse` / `csv_emit` — Char-stream structural walks over the
//   RFC 4180 grammar parameterized by `CsvDialect`; require std/text.dag
//   `String` ↔ `List<Char>` decomposition (T-3). RATIFIED strategy:
//   single-pass scan with quoted-field sub-machine (RFC 4180 §2) —
//   termination is input consumption (A2 implicit), not a ranking measure.
//     (a) scaffold doc — THIS note; no `csv_parse`/`csv_emit` in this file.
//     (b) bounds on use — no consumer reads these symbols here yet.
//         When authored: `csv_parse` / `csv_emit` use `Outcome<…>` fail-
//         closed BOTH directions (json.dag #3184 seam #3).
//     (c) dissolution trigger — std/text.dag Char-stream substrate lands
//         ⇒ follow-up adds the operations + fail-closed emit validation
//         (invalid dialect pairing, non-Unicode-scalar `Char` in dialect,
//         rectangularity violations, etc.).
//
// (2) RFC 4180 rectangularity + `width` consistency — PARSE/VALIDATION
//   authority until the length-indexed row substrate lands (see modeling
//   notes). The deferred `csv_parse` computes `width` from the header row
//   (if `HeaderPresent`) or the first data row (`HeaderAbsent`), then
//   rejects any row with a different field count as `Rejected { diagnostic
//   }` — never pads, never truncates, never normalizes away the mismatch
//   (INVARIANTS P3). **Named trigger:** operator pins structural
//   `Row<W>` / `PositiveInt` width refinement + immutable-header reconcile
//   (csv-class) ⇒ illegal ragged states become unrepresentable at the type
//   layer and this deferral dissolves.
